Ore grade

 and other properties (for example, the color for the limestone) might be critical.

There are multiple points in the mining process ("streams") where the grade ("assay") can be valued:

assayed grade (also measured grade, in-situ grade) is the result of assaying of the ore body;

head grade is the grade of the ore as removed from the mine (and delivered to the ore mill as feed grade). Head grade is lower than the assayed one, as the mining process frequently includes the wall rock (overburden) with the extracted ore (so called mining dilution);

concentrate grade is the grade of the output of the mill, limited by the chemical composition of the ore. For example, if the ore contains copper only as chalcopyrite (CuFeS), the attainable grade is no more than 34.6%. The ratio between the amount of commodity in the feed and in the concentrate is called recovery. For example, 90% recovery of a metal indicates that 10% were "rejected", sent by the mill into the tailings along with the gangue.
